<section class="col-xs-12 col-sm-12 col-md-12 col-lg-9 col-xl-9" id="main">
<div class="html" id="tag">
<h3 class="tex-subsection" id="subsection:weak-locality"><span data-tag="04LG">9.6.2</span> Weakly Local Objects</h3>
<p>
Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a category and let $W$ be a collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Recall that an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $W$-local (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="02FZ" href="/tag/02FZ">6.2.3.1</a>) if, for every morphism $f: C \rightarrow X$ in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and every morphism $w: C \rightarrow D$ which belongs to $W$, there is a unique morphism $g: D \rightarrow Y$ satisfying $g \circ w = f$, as indicated in the diagram 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@C =50pt@R=50pt{ &amp;  D \ar@ {--&gt;}[dr]^{g} &amp;  \\ C \ar [ur]^{w} \ar [rr]^{f} &amp;  &amp;  X. } \]
</div>
<p>
 It will sometimes be useful to consider the following weaker condition: 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-definition" id="04LH">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04LH">Definition <span data-tag="04LH">9.6.2.1</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a category and let $w: C \rightarrow D$ be a morphism of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. We say that an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is <em>weakly $w$-local</em> if, for every morphism $f: C \rightarrow X$ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, there exists a morphism $g: D \rightarrow X$ satisfying $g \circ w = f$. If $W$ is a collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, we say that $X$ is <em>weakly $W$-local</em> if it is weakly $w$-local for each $w \in W$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="04LJ">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04LJ">Example <span data-tag="04LJ">9.6.2.2</span> <span class="named">(Kan Complexes)</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}= \operatorname{Set_{\Delta }}$ be the category of simplicial sets and let $W$ be the collection of all horn inclusions $\Lambda ^{n}_{i} \hookrightarrow \Delta ^ n$, where $n &gt; 0$ and $0 \leq i \leq n$. Then a simplicial set is weakly $W$-local if and only if it is a Kan complex. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="04LK">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04LK">Example <span data-tag="04LK">9.6.2.3</span> <span class="named">($\infty $-Categories)</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}= \operatorname{Set_{\Delta }}$ be the category of simplicial sets and let $W$ be the collection of all inner horn inclusions $\Lambda ^{n}_{i} \hookrightarrow \Delta ^ n$, where $0 &lt; i &lt; n$. Then a simplicial set is weakly $W$-local if and only if it is an $\infty $-category. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="04LL">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04LL">Example <span data-tag="04LL">9.6.2.4</span> <span class="named">(Contractible Kan Complexes)</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}= \operatorname{Set_{\Delta }}$ be the category of simplicial sets and let $W$ be the collection of inclusion maps $\operatorname{\partial \Delta }^{n} \hookrightarrow \Delta ^{n}$. Then a simplicial set is weakly $W$-local if and only if it is a contractible Kan complex. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
Definition <a data-tag="04LH" href="/tag/04LH">9.6.2.1</a> has an obvious counterpart in the setting of $\infty $-categories: 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-definition" id="04LM">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04LM">Definition <span data-tag="04LM">9.6.2.5</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $w: C \rightarrow D$ be a morphism of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. We say that an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is <em>weakly $w$-local</em> if, for every morphism $f: C \rightarrow X$, there exists a $2$-simplex with boundary indicated in the diagram </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@C =50pt@R=50pt{ &amp;  D \ar@ {--&gt;}[dr] &amp;  \\ C \ar [ur]^{w} \ar [rr]^{f} &amp;  &amp;  X. } \]
</div>
<p> If $W$ is a collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, we say that an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is <em>weakly $W$-local</em> if it is weakly $w$-local for each $w \in W$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="04LP">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04LP">Remark <span data-tag="04LP">9.6.2.6</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $W$ be a collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. It follows from Proposition <a data-tag="0043" href="/tag/0043">1.4.4.2</a> that an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is weakly $W$-local if and only if, for every morphism $w: C \rightarrow D$ which belongs to $W$, composition with the homotopy class $[w]$ induces a surjection $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}( D, X ) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}(C,X)$. In other words, the object $X$ is weakly $W$-local (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="04LM" href="/tag/04LM">9.6.2.5</a>) if and only if it is weakly $[W]$-local when regarded as an object of the homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="04LH" href="/tag/04LH">9.6.2.1</a>). Here $[W] = \{  [w]: w \in W \} $ denotes the collection of all homotopy classes of morphisms which belong to $W$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="04LN">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04LN">Example <span data-tag="04LN">9.6.2.7</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a category and let $W$ be a collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Then an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is weakly $W$-local (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="04LH" href="/tag/04LH">9.6.2.1</a>) if and only if it is weakly $W$-local when regarded as an object of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="04LM" href="/tag/04LM">9.6.2.5</a>). </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="04LR">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04LR">Remark <span data-tag="04LR">9.6.2.8</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $w: X \rightarrow Y$ a morphism of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which admits a left homotopy inverse $r: Y \rightarrow X$. Then every object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is weakly $w$-local. In particular, if $w$ is an isomorphism, then every object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is weakly $w$-local. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="04LQ">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04LQ">Example <span data-tag="04LQ">9.6.2.9</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $W$ be a collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. If an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $W$-local (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="02FZ" href="/tag/02FZ">6.2.3.1</a>), then it is weakly $W$-local (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="04LM" href="/tag/04LM">9.6.2.5</a>). </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="04LT">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04LT">Remark <span data-tag="04LT">9.6.2.10</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category, let $W$ be a collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, and let $\{  C_ i \} _{i \in I}$ be a collection of objects of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which admit a product $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (see Definition <a data-tag="03EC" href="/tag/03EC">7.6.1.3</a>). If each $C_ i$ is weakly $W$-local, then $C$ is weakly $W$-local. In particular, any final object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is weakly $W$-local. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="04LU">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04LU">Remark <span data-tag="04LU">9.6.2.11</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category, let $W$ be a collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, and let $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an object. If $X$ is weakly $W$-local, then any retract of $X$ is also weakly $W$-local. Similarly, if $X$ is $W$-local, then any retract of $X$ is $W$-local. In particular, the condition that $X$ is (weakly) $W$-local depends only on the isomorphism class of $X$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-variant" id="04LV">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04LV">Variant <span data-tag="04LV">9.6.2.12</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category, let $w: C \rightarrow D$ and $w': C' \rightarrow D'$ be morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, and suppose that $w'$ is a retract of $w$ (in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$). If an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is weakly $w$-local, then it is also weakly $w'$-local. Similarly, if $X$ is $w$-local, then it is also $w'$-local. In particular, if we regard the object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ as fixed, then the condition that $X$ is (weakly) $w$-local depends only on the isomorphism class of $w$ (as an object of $\operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$). </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="04LW">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04LW">Proposition <span data-tag="04LW">9.6.2.13</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category containing a pushout diagram </p>
<div class="equation" id="06VY">
<span class="equation-label"><a data-tag="06VY" href="/tag/06VY">9.19</a></span>
<div>\begin{equation}  \begin{gathered}\label{equation:pushout-of-semiorthogonal} \xymatrix@C =50pt@R=50pt{ C \ar [d]^{w} \ar [r] &amp;  C' \ar [d]^{w'} \\ D \ar [r] &amp;  D'. } \end{gathered} \end{equation}</div>
</div>
<p> If an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is weakly $w$-local, then it is also weakly $w'$-local. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      We have a commutative diagram of sets 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@C =30pt@R=50pt{ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}( D', X ) \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}( C', X) \times _{ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}( C, X ) } \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}( D, X ) \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}( C', X) \ar [d] \\ &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}(D,X) \ar [r]^-{ \circ [w] } &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}( C, X), } \]
</div>
<p>
       where the square on the right is a pullback. Our assumption that $X$ is weakly $w$-local guarantees that the bottom horizontal map is surjective, so that the upper horizontal map on the right is surjective. Since (<a data-tag="06VY" href="/tag/06VY">9.19</a>) is a pushout square, the horizontal map on the upper left is also surjective (Warning <a data-tag="03FH" href="/tag/03FH">7.6.2.3</a>). It follows that the composite map $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}( D', X) \xrightarrow { \circ [w'] } \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}(C',X)$ is surjective. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></article>
<p>
Example <a data-tag="04LQ" href="/tag/04LQ">9.6.2.9</a> admits the following partial converse: 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="04LY">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04LY">Proposition <span data-tag="04LY">9.6.2.14</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $W$ be a collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Suppose that every morphism $w: C \rightarrow D$ of $W$ admits a relative codiagonal $\gamma _{C/D}: D \coprod _{C} D \rightarrow D$ which also belongs to $W$ (Variant <a data-tag="04K7" href="/tag/04K7">7.6.2.23</a>). Then an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $W$-local if and only if it is weakly $W$-local. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Fix an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, and let $h_{X}: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ denote the functor represented by $X$. We wish to show that, for every morphism $w: C \rightarrow D$ which belongs to $W$, the image $h_{X}(w)$ is a homotopy equivalence of Kan complexes. By virtue of Remark <a data-tag="04HJ" href="/tag/04HJ">3.5.1.19</a>, it will suffice to show that $h_{X}(w)$ is $n$-connective for every integer $n \geq 0$. The proof proceeds by induction on $n$. In the case $n = 0$, we wish to show that the composition map $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(D,X) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,X)$ is surjective on connected components, which follows from our assumption that $X$ is weakly $W$-local. Let us therefore assume that $n &gt; 0$. Using the criterion of Corollary <a data-tag="04HA" href="/tag/04HA">3.5.1.29</a> (together with Exercise <a data-tag="04K8" href="/tag/04K8">7.6.3.13</a>), we are reduced to proving the $(n-1)$-connectivity of the relative diagonal of $h_{X}(w)$ (formed in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$). Since the functor $h_{X}$ preserves limits (Proposition <a data-tag="039L" href="/tag/039L">7.4.1.22</a>), we can identify the relative diagonal of $h_{X}(w)$ with $h_{X}( \gamma _{C/D} )$, where $\gamma _{C/D}: D \coprod _{C} D \rightarrow D$ denotes a relative codiagonal of $w$. By assumption, we can arrange that $\gamma _{C/D}$ is also contained in $W$, so the desired result follows from our inductive hypothesis. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-exercise" id="04LZ">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04LZ">Exercise <span data-tag="04LZ">9.6.2.15</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $w: C \rightarrow D$ be a morphism of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which admits a relative codiagonal $\gamma _{C/D}: D \coprod _{C} D \rightarrow D$. Show that an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $w$-local if and only if it is both $\gamma _{C/D}$-local and weakly $w$-local. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="04M0">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04M0">Proposition <span data-tag="04M0">9.6.2.16</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category, let $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an object, and let $W$ be the collection of morphisms $w: C \rightarrow C'$ in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ such that $X$ is weakly $w$-local. Then $W$ is closed under transfinite composition (see Definition <a data-tag="04L0" href="/tag/04L0">9.6.1.1</a>). </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be the projection map and let $f: C \rightarrow C'$ be a morphism of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which can be written as a transfinite composition of morphisms of $W$. Suppose we are given a morphism $C \rightarrow X$ in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, which we identify with an object $\widetilde{C} \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X}$ satisfying $U( \widetilde{C} ) = C$. We wish to show that there is a morphism $\widetilde{f}: \widetilde{C} \rightarrow \widetilde{C}'$ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X}$ satisfying $U( \widetilde{f} ) = f$. 
    <p>
      Choose an ordinal $\alpha $ and a functor $F: \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{ \leq \alpha } ) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which exhibits $f$ as a transfinite composition of morphisms of $W$. Let $Q$ be the collection of all ordered pairs $(\beta , \widetilde{F}_{\leq \beta } )$, where $\beta \leq \alpha $ is an ordinal and $\widetilde{F}_{\leq \beta }: \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{\leq \beta } ) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X}$ is a functor satisfying $\widetilde{F}_{\leq \beta }(0) = \widetilde{X}$ and $U \circ \widetilde{F}_{\leq \beta } = F|_{ \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{\leq \beta } ) }$. We regard $Q$ as a partially ordered set, where $(\beta , \widetilde{F}_{\leq \beta } ) \leq (\beta ', \widetilde{F}'|_{\leq \beta '})$ if $\beta \leq \beta '$ and $\widetilde{F}_{\leq \beta } = \widetilde{F}'_{\leq \beta '} |_{ \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{\leq \beta } ) }$. 
    <p>
      We first claim that $Q$ satisfies the hypotheses of Zorn's lemma. Let $Q_0 \subseteq Q$ be a linearly ordered subset of $Q$; we wish to show that $Q_0$ admits an upper bound. If $Q_0$ is empty, we can take this upper bound be the pair $(0, \widetilde{F}_{\leq 0} )$, where $\widetilde{F}_{\leq 0}$ is the constant functor taking the value $\widetilde{X}$. Without loss of generality, we may assume that $Q_0$ does not contain a maximal element (otherwise, there is nothing to prove). Write $Q_0 = \{  ( \beta _ i, \widetilde{F}_{\leq \beta _ i} ) \} _{i \in I}$ and let $\beta \leq \alpha $ be the supremum of the set $\{  \beta _ i \} _{i \in I}$. The functors $\widetilde{F}_{\leq \beta _ i}$ can then be amalgamated to a single functor $\widetilde{F}_{&lt; \beta }: \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{&lt; \beta } ) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X}$. To find an upper bound for $Q_0$, it will suffice to show that the lifting problem 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@C =50pt@R=50pt{ \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{&lt; \beta } ) \ar [r]^-{ \widetilde{F}_{&lt; \beta } } \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X} \ar [d]^{U} \\ \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{ \leq \beta } ) \ar [r]^-{ F|_{ \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{\leq \beta } ) } } &amp;  \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} \]
</div>
<p>
       admits a solution. This follows immediately from our assumption that $F|_{ \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{\leq \beta } ) }$ is a colimit diagram in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. 
    <p>
      Applying Zorn's lemma, we deduce that $Q$ contains a maximal element $(\beta , \widetilde{F}_{\leq \beta } )$. To complete the proof, it will suffice to show that $\beta = \alpha $; we can then take $\widetilde{f}$ to be obtained by applying the functor $\widetilde{F}$ to the edge of $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{\leq \alpha } )$ given by the pair $(0, \alpha )$. Assume otherwise, let $F_{\leq \beta }$ denote the restriction $F|_{ \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{\leq \beta } )}$, and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ denote the coslice $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ F_{\leq \beta } / }$. Then $F_{\leq \beta +1}$ and $\widetilde{F}_{\leq \beta }$ can be identified with objects $D,D' \in \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$, and the maximality of $(\beta , \widetilde{F}_{\leq \beta } )$ guarantees that $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}( D, D' ) = \emptyset $. Since the inclusion map $\{ \beta \}  \hookrightarrow \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{\leq \beta } )$ is right anodyne (Corollary <a data-tag="02J5" href="/tag/02J5">4.7.3.22</a>), the restriction map $V: \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}= \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ F_{\leq \beta } / } \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ F(\beta ) / }$ is a trivial Kan fibration (Corollary <a data-tag="018L" href="/tag/018L">4.3.6.14</a>). It follows that the morphism space $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ F(\beta ) / } }( V(D), V(D') )$ is also empty: that is, there is no $2$-simplex of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ with boundary indicated in the diagram 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@C =50pt@R=50pt{ &amp;  F(\beta +1) \ar@ {--&gt;}[dr] &amp;  \\ F(\beta ) \ar [ur] \ar [rr] &amp;  &amp;  X. } \]
</div>
<p>
       This contradicts our assumption that the morphism $F(\beta ) \rightarrow F(\beta +1)$ belongs to $W$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></p></p></p></p></p></article>
